# Baki GO — Project Rules

## Product Context

Baki GO is a **Business Operating System for Network Marketing organizations** — not a CRM, KPI dashboard, or task manager. Before implementing features, read the relevant docs in `/docs`.

## Golden Rule

> Every member should know exactly what to do next.

## Non-Negotiable Principles

### Mobile-first

Everything must be designed for phones first. Desktop is secondary.

### Enter data once

Users must never re-enter the same information. Every statistic is calculated automatically from stored activity.

### Motivation first

Every screen should increase motivation. Every interaction should make users want to come back tomorrow.

### Business before technology

Business rules always come first. Technology exists only to support the business. Never hardcode business logic — rules belong in `docs/BUSINESS_RULES.md`.

### Priority 0 — Rule Engine (non-negotiable)

- **Never infer KPI numbers in UI or engines.** Any target (10, 100, 500, 2500, etc.) must be defined in Business Rules and returned by the Rule Engine.
- If a required rule is missing or its target is `null`, return **Rule Missing** / **等待使用者定義。** — do not show placeholder KPIs or default targets.
- UI is a renderer only. Computing `(current / target) * 100` or similar in components is a bug; progress must come from engine output.
- Violations of this rule are bugs, not acceptable shortcuts.

### Simplicity

Usable by someone who has never used CRM software. If a feature feels complicated, redesign it.

### Gamification is core

Progress should feel like a game. Members earn levels, experience, badges, achievements, titles, and rankings. Consult `docs/GAME_DESIGN.md` before building user-facing flows.

### Meeting friendly

Support weekly team meetings. Members should review progress together on one screen.

### Leader first

Leaders must instantly see who needs help, who is improving, who is falling behind, and who deserves recognition.

### Data driven

All coaching is based on real logged activity — never memory or assumptions.

### Long-term thinking

Every feature should still make sense five years from now. Avoid shortcuts. Build systems.

### UI inspiration

Follow `docs/UI_SYSTEM.md`: clean and spacious (Apple), progress-driven and celebratory (Apple Fitness), fast and minimal (Linear).

## Career Path

Members progress: New Member → Supervisor → Active Supervisor → World Team → President. Rank rules live in `docs/BUSINESS_RULES.md`.

## Code Standards

- **Readability and scalability** over cleverness.
- **Never hardcode business logic** — implement what the docs define.
- When behavior is unclear, update documentation first, then code.

